(project pricklypear
 (doc "A single-tenant Lisp operating system in the Smalltalk tradition: one OCaml `unix' binary (the image), Postgres as the durable object store, htmx as the interface. Formerly multi-user + MirageOS unikernels; pivoted to single-image in it35 (see single-image.borg).")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|it31 plan-pass #7 (product-truth reconciliation):
  Demoted project status implemented -> partial. The substrate
  (5 packages, 3 migrations, scripts/dev.sh, 92 alcotest cases,
  end-to-end smoke path SSH/line-protocol -> authd -> image ->
  REPL -> define -> call) IS real and green. That is not the same
  as the v1 product experience bar. Project stays partial until a
  user can: (1) log in without developer ceremony, (2) use base
  functions in a live image, (3) create/edit functions from the
  environment (not only raw REPL typing), and (4) optionally accept
  AI-proposed functions on a branch. New planned product surfaces
  added this pass: auth.login-experience, auth.web-login,
  auth.user-management, image.system-browser, image.browser-repl,
  image.function-editor, image.inspector, ai-authoring.*, and
  branching.ast-nodes. Systems sections remain mostly partial/
  implemented as before; type-system stays planned (typed fields
  deferred). Solo5 target remains a v1.5 hardening axis.|) *)
 (verify
  (build "dune build @all — exit 0 across all 5 packages + spikes/awa_ssh")
  (test "dune runtest — 92 alcotest cases pass (lisp 30 + common 10 + auth 4 + image 48)")
  (smoke "scripts/dev.sh start boots PG + authd + supervisor; ssh admin@host -p 17822 → \"hello\" from the image REPL")
  (smoke "v1 product cut (not yet): login + base fns + define/edit in browser + optional AI propose on branch"))
 (convention ocaml-dune
 )
 (no-inline
 )
 (roerick
  (|
   Pricklypear is an operating system in the Smalltalk tradition:
   a live, inspectable image where programs are editable data
   and the running state IS the system. It is NOT Smalltalk:
   no OOP, no image snapshots, no single-address-space-only
   single-user world.

   It is built on three architectural pillars:

   1. MirageOS unikernels. The "OS" is OCaml code linked into
      a unikernel. No userspace/kernel split, no syscalls.
      Everything is inspectable OCaml values. The unikernel
      boots in ~2s, making "rebuild the world" a cheap
      operation and A/B rollback viable as a routine
      operation, not a disaster-recovery one.

   2. Postgres as the object store. Durable state (function
      definitions, type schemas, branch metadata, user
      records, application data) lives in Postgres. The
      image is ephemeral and rebuildable from the store.
      This splits durability from execution cleanly.

   3. A Lisp interpreter (Scheme-flavored, Lisp-1, lexically
      scoped). Programmers write functions in Lisp; the AST
      IS the source (homoiconicity); functions are stored
      as JSONB rows in Postgres, editable live without
      rebuilding the image. The OCaml layer (primitives,
      interpreter, transports) changes slowly via
      supervised A/B boot. The Lisp layer changes fast via
      row updates in the store.

   Two unikernels compose the runtime:

   - The SUPERVISOR unikernel: booted directly by the
     hypervisor, manages a fleet of per-user IMAGE
     unikernels, handles A/B rollback and health checks,
     serves a minimal admin HTTP surface. Targets Solo5
     in production, mirage/unix in dev.

   - The IMAGE unikernel: booted per-user-session, runs
     the Lisp interpreter, exposes an internal REPL
     endpoint (supervisor fronts SSH and forwards) and
     an htmx HTTP endpoint. Runs as mirage/unix initially;
     v1 uses httpaf-lwt-unix + pure-html + pgx_lwt
     (+ Io_unix). Solo5 is a later build-axis swap
     (gluten-mirage + httpaf + pgx_lwt_mirage), not a
     rewrite of the Lisp/store model.

   The hypervisor box runs: the supervisor unikernel,
   Postgres, and the per-user image unikernels the
   supervisor spawns. Nothing else. There is no OpenBSD
   userland in the hot path; the hypervisor trusts exactly
   one thing (the supervisor) and the supervisor manages
   the rest.

   Multi-user is achieved by process isolation: each user
   gets their own image instance. Namespaces, budgets, and
   author-runs-as-author permissions handle the remaining
   concerns. Code is branched (git-style fork/merge) so
   users can experiment without clobbering each other or
   the system baseline ('main').

   This file is the source of truth. If the implementation
   disagrees, the implementation is wrong.
  |)
 )
